{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":375676,"duration_api_ms":420181,"num_turns":44,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a39a9107-a52a-47e2-9e74-2152014f6b9f","total_cost_usd":1.9156005000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":29,"cache_creation_input_tokens":79079,"cache_read_input_tokens":1590476,"output_tokens":14743,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":79079,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":33,"outputTokens":15426,"cacheReadInputTokens":1615798,"cacheCreationInputTokens":96222,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.7951015000000006,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":79,"outputTokens":6288,"cacheReadInputTokens":282100,"cacheCreationInputTokens":48616,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.120499,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"All five fixture functions (breeding_success_state, thermal_scenario_state, chemistry_stress_state, chloride_test_base_state, crash_mode_state) manually override DIC, alkalinity, and bicarbonate_mg_total after calling WaterState::default_for_volume_l, but never re-solve carbonate equilibrium before calling reseed_stability_tracker(). This leaves state.water.ph stale (derived from the default DIC=20/alk=1.5 chemistry, roughly pH 7.3) while the actual overridden chemistry (e.g., DIC=5/alk=12 in breeding_success_state) would resolve to a significantly different pH (likely >8.5). On the first hourly tick, apply_hourly_chemistry_terms calls resolve_carbonate_state, correcting pH — but the stability tracker's prev_ph still holds the stale value, causing a false instability signal from the pH discontinuity. Fix: insert a resolve_carbonate_state(&mut state.water, vol) call after setting custom DIC/alkalinity/bicarbonate and before reseed_stability_tracker() in each fixture function. This ensures the initial state is internally consistent and the stability tracker baseline matches the actual chemistry.","rationale":"The breeding probe asserts peak_instability < 0.35, which passes today but the false pH swing from the fixture inconsistency consumes instability budget for a non-physical reason. A parameter retune that widens the DIC/alkalinity gap or tightens the instability threshold could break the test for the wrong reason — the fixture setup rather than the simulation behavior. This is exactly the kind of brittleness the acceptance criteria warn against ('later retuning can preserve intent without freezing every exact count')."}],"summary":"Reviewed the full 1417-line e2e_shrimp_scenario_probes.rs test file and its integration with the harness, shrimp system, and chemistry subsystems. All 5 probes compile and pass (6/6 tests green including the summary runner). The test design is solid: clear husbandry stories in doc comments, directional/envelope assertions resilient to retuning, comprehensive shrimp_diag() dumps on failure, structured tracing enabled for every probe, and the summary runner exits non-zero on envelope violation. The breeding probe runs 1440 hours (exceeds 1000h requirement) and observes 10 complete reproductive cycles (exceeds 2-cycle requirement). The chloride protection probe uses a well-designed branching approach (shared pre-treatment phase, then forked treated/control arms). One correctness concern: all fixture functions create internally inconsistent initial carbonate state (manually set DIC/alkalinity/bicarbonate without re-solving pH), causing a false instability signal on the first tick that consumes stability budget. The complete_cycle_count metric (line 282) uses unique event days rather than paired berried→hatch sequences, making it a loose but conservative proxy — adequate for envelope testing."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"11058f54-3e68-49a4-87da-d68bbdb70a45"}
